@node Differential Operators
@section The differential operators

A differential program is operators applied to collections, yielding
collections to which more operators apply.  This section surveys the
common ones.  Each takes a @code{dtfl_collection} and returns a new one;
the caller frees the handles with @code{dtfl_collection_free}.

@subsection Map, filter, flat_map

@code{dtfl_collection_map} transforms the record of each update, keeping
its time and diff; @code{dtfl_collection_flat_map} emits zero or more
records per input; @code{dtfl_collection_filter} keeps the updates whose
record satisfies a predicate.  Because a record is a byte string, map
and flat_map take the output record type explicitly and write each
output through an emit handle (as @code{swap_pair} did in
@ref{Scaling Up}).

@subsection Concat and consolidate

@code{dtfl_collection_concat} (and @code{dtfl_collection_concatenate}
for an array) accumulates the updates of several collections into one.
Concatenation does not combine updates, so a record can appear with
several diffs at one time.  @code{dtfl_collection_consolidate} groups
updates by @code{(record, time)}, sums their diffs, and drops those that
sum to zero, exchanging records so each @code{(record, time)} is
accumulated on one worker.  It is how a program renders a collection in
its simplest form: at most one update per record per time.
@code{dtfl_collection_negate} flips every diff's sign (its diff class
must be Abelian), which is how a program retracts a whole collection.

@subsection Join

@code{dtfl_collection_join} matches @code{(key, val1)} against
@code{(key, val2)} by key and emits @code{key || val1 || val2}.
@code{dtfl_collection_join_map} applies a closure to each matched
@code{(key, val1, val2)} and emits whatever record the closure writes
(the key is @emph{not} prepended automatically, so the skip-level
closure wrote it itself, @pxref{Scaling Up}).  The diff of each output is
the product of the two input diffs, so the two diff classes must
multiply, or join reports @code{DTFL_EALGEBRA}.
@code{dtfl_collection_semijoin} keeps the records of one collection whose
key appears in another; @code{dtfl_collection_antijoin} keeps those
whose key does not (its diff class must be Abelian).

@subsection Reduce

@code{dtfl_collection_reduce} groups a collection of @code{key || val}
records by key and applies a reduction to the accumulated multiset of
values at each key.  It is incremental: when a key is re-evaluated at a
new time, the previous output is retracted and the new one asserted, so
the output is always the function of the input at every time.  The logic
receives the key and the values @emph{sorted by the value type's order}
(@pxref{Records}), which is why a minimum reducer simply emits the first
value:

@example
static void
min_managee(void *ctx, const void *key, size_t klen, size_t n,
    const void *const *vals, const size_t *vlens, const void *diffs,
    dtfl_reduce_emit *out)
@{
        int64_t one = 1;
        unsigned char vbuf[8];
        dtfl_put_u64(vbuf, dtfl_get_u64(vals[0]));   /* the smallest */
        dtfl_reduce_emit_pair(out, vbuf, sizeof vbuf, &one);
@}
@end example

The complete program is in @file{doc/snippets/dd_reduce.c}: for each
manager, the managee with the lowest identifier.  It prints
@code{manager 0 -> managee 0}, @code{manager 1 -> managee 2}, and so on.
@code{count}, @code{distinct} and @code{threshold} are the common
special cases of reduce, with total-order fast paths
(@code{count_total}, @code{distinct_total}, @code{threshold_total}) for
a totally ordered time.

@subsection Iterate

@code{dtfl_collection_iterate} applies a closure to a collection
repeatedly until the result stops changing (a fixed point).  It builds
an iterative subscope whose time adds a @code{u64} iteration counter
(@pxref{Concepts}).  The transitive closure of the management relation,
for example, extends the relation one hop at a time:

@example
/* inside the iterate logic, over the collection "transitive": */
swap   = dtfl_collection_map(transitive, pair, swap_pair, DTFL_NOCTX);
hop    = dtfl_collection_join_map(swap, transitive, u64, u64, u64,
             pair, drop_middle, DTFL_NOCTX);
grown  = dtfl_collection_concat(hop, transitive);
return dtfl_collection_distinct(grown, i64);
@end example

Three details make it converge.  The @code{map} drops the middle manager
so the result has the same shape as the input; the @code{concat} keeps
the shorter relations so no hop is lost; and the @code{distinct} at the
end bounds the multiplicities, so the loop winds down once no new pairs
appear.  A collection iterate is called on must have an Abelian diff
class (it negates the source); @code{dtfl_variable_new} and friends let
you write mutual recursion with several collections evolving together.

@subsection Arrange

Several operators do the same thing with their input: they build an
index of the updates.  @code{dtfl_collection_arrange} and
@code{dtfl_collection_arrange_by_key} build that index once and share it,
so join and reduce can read a shared @dfn{arrangement} rather than each
re-indexing.  Arrangements are the subject of their own section
(@pxref{Arrangements}).
